# Cedar of Lebanon: ONE crown over the whole tree's height (Shape Cone: the longest limbs low, ever shorter up the tree). A massive
# buttressed trunk carries long, thick FALLING limbs (Angle 104 at the crown's bottom: 14 degrees down, from a quarter
# of the height; their tips curve back up, Module UpAttract 0.18) up to the fork at 45% of its height (Trunk Fork),
# where it splits into 3-4 gnarled stems leaning a little apart, layered with ever shorter, level limbs (Angle 88 at
# the top) and each ENDING in a small flat sheet (Crown Top, no leader). Every limb carries a FOLIAGE PAD (Leaves Pad):
# big, mostly level needle-spray cards scattered through a flat dome over it - the tiers read as clouds of foliage
# with the branches inside them. Tiers at uneven heights (Crown Tiers with a jitter). Grey, scaly bark. Dry temperate
# mountains.
